From 744a7fee14e7b4b8060261667e510e9ba47a660a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The browser Authors <noreply@browser.invalid>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:39:50 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 23/61] software-playready

Software-secure PlayReady (SL2000) through the OS Media Foundation CDM for
allow-listed origins (primevideo.com and amazon.com by default;
--software-playready-sites=a,b extends the list, * allows every origin).
Every other origin keeps stock Chromium's NotSupported answer, so the EME
surface is unchanged elsewhere.

In a Linux build presenting Windows, PlayReady's probes are answered as Windows answers them: the recommendation key system with hardware secure H.264 and AAC (cenc, cbcs), with no CDM behind it, so createMediaKeys() fails as for any key system whose CDM is missing. PlayReadyKeySystemInfo is compiled on Linux for it, and the protected media identifier its configuration needs is granted in the renderer, as Windows grants it in a normal profile: Linux's browser has no such permission. The first EME call of a browser session waits as long as Windows' Media Foundation capability query (1.5 to 2.3 s; stock Chrome 156 on Windows 11 takes 1.7 s) instead of answering in a millisecond.
